A boolean array is enough here. `dp[i]` means that the prefix `s[0:i]` can be made from dictionary words. For every ending position, I try each possible earlier split and check the remaining part in a set.

For `s = "leetcode"`, the useful positions become `0`, `4`, and `8`. The word `leet` makes `dp[4]` true, then `code` makes `dp[8]` true.

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <unordered_set>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    bool wordBreak(string s, vector<string>& wordDict) {
        int n = static_cast<int>(s.size());
        unordered_set<string> words(wordDict.begin(), wordDict.end());
        vector<bool> dp(n + 1, false);
        dp[0] = true;

        for (int end = 1; end <= n; ++end) {
            for (int start = 0; start < end; ++start) {
                if (dp[start] && words.count(s.substr(start, end - start))) {
                    dp[end] = true;
                    break;
                }
            }
        }

        return dp[n];
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n^3)` in the usual string-copy model, because there are `O(n^2)` checks and making a substring can cost `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n + d)`, where `d` is the total size of the words stored in the set.

This is the direct recursive version. At each position, it grows a prefix and recurses whenever that prefix is in the dictionary. It returns as soon as one complete split is found.

There is no memoization in this code, so the same suffix may be checked repeatedly. That makes it easy to follow, but it can become slow when many different splits lead to the same failed suffix.

The important edge cases are covered: reaching the end returns `true`, while a string with no matching prefix returns `false`. A dictionary word longer than the remaining string is harmless because its built prefix can never match.

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <unordered_set>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    bool wordBreak(string s, vector<string>& wordDict) {
        unordered_set<string> words(wordDict.begin(), wordDict.end());
        return search(s, 0, words);
    }

private:
    bool search(const string& s, int start, const unordered_set<string>& words) {
        if (start == static_cast<int>(s.size())) {
            return true;
        }

        string prefix;
        for (int end = start; end < static_cast<int>(s.size()); ++end) {
            prefix.push_back(s[end]);
            if (words.count(prefix) && search(s, end + 1, words)) {
                return true;
            }
        }

        return false;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(2^n)` in the worst case from trying many possible splits, with additional substring or hashing costs.
Space complexity: `O(n + d)` for the recursion depth, temporary prefix, and dictionary set.

A trie lets the code read the string one character at a time instead of checking every dictionary word at each position. Whenever a trie node marks the end of a word, the remaining suffix becomes a smaller subproblem.

The `can_build` cache stores the answer for each starting index. The input is not changed.

A small dry run with `s = "applepenapple"` first finds `apple`, then `pen`, and finally the last `apple`, so the answer is `True`.

An empty dictionary also works safely here because the trie simply has no children, and a non-empty string returns `False`.

```python
class TrieNode:
    def __init__(self):
        self.children = {}
        self.is_word = False


class Solution:
    def wordBreak(self, s: str, wordDict: list[str]) -> bool:
        root = TrieNode()

        for word in wordDict:
            node = root
            for ch in word:
                if ch not in node.children:
                    node.children[ch] = TrieNode()
                node = node.children[ch]
            node.is_word = True

        n = len(s)
        memo = [None] * (n + 1)

        def can_build(start: int) -> bool:
            if start == n:
                return True
            if memo[start] is not None:
                return memo[start]

            node = root
            for end in range(start, n):
                ch = s[end]
                if ch not in node.children:
                    break

                node = node.children[ch]
                if node.is_word and can_build(end + 1):
                    memo[start] = True
                    return True

            memo[start] = False
            return False

        return can_build(0)
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2 + d)`, where `d` is the total number of characters in the dictionary. Each starting index scans forward through the string, and trie insertion takes `O(d)`.
Space complexity: `O(n + d)` for the memo array, recursion stack, and trie.

The recursive function starts at an index and asks whether the suffix from there can be completed. Every possible dictionary word is tried at that position. Results are saved, so a suffix that already failed is not searched again.

This is a top-down version, so it can stop early as soon as it finds a valid ending. The cache also handles cases with many overlapping choices.

```java
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Set;

class Solution {
    public boolean wordBreak(String s, List<String> wordDict) {
        Set<String> words = new HashSet<>(wordDict);
        Boolean[] memo = new Boolean[s.length() + 1];
        return canBuild(s, 0, words, memo);
    }

    private boolean canBuild(String s, int start, Set<String> words, Boolean[] memo) {
        if (start == s.length()) {
            return true;
        }
        if (memo[start] != null) {
            return memo[start];
        }

        for (int end = start + 1; end <= s.length(); ++end) {
            String part = s.substring(start, end);
            if (words.contains(part) && canBuild(s, end, words, memo)) {
                memo[start] = true;
                return true;
            }
        }

        memo[start] = false;
        return false;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n^3)` in the worst case when substring creation is counted.
Space complexity: `O(n + d)`, including the memo array, recursion stack, and dictionary set.

I treated each position between characters as a point in a graph. If a dictionary word matches from `start` to `end`, the search can move from `start` to `end`. Reaching `s.length` means the whole string was formed.

The visited array is important. Without it, the same position could be added to the queue many times.

For `s = "catsanddog"`, the queue can reach position `3` using `cat`, then position `7` using `sand`, and finally position `10` using `dog`.

```javascript
class Solution {
    wordBreak(s, wordDict) {
        const words = new Set(wordDict);
        const queue = [0];
        const visited = new Array(s.length + 1).fill(false);
        visited[0] = true;

        let head = 0;
        while (head < queue.length) {
            const start = queue[head++];
            if (start === s.length) {
                return true;
            }

            for (let end = start + 1; end <= s.length; ++end) {
                if (visited[end]) {
                    continue;
                }

                if (words.has(s.slice(start, end))) {
                    visited[end] = true;
                    queue.push(end);
                }
            }
        }

        return false;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n^3)` in the worst case because slicing can copy up to `n` characters for each pair of positions.
Space complexity: `O(n + d)` for the queue, visited positions, and word set.

